<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chagatai Khanate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chagatai Empire, Chagataid, Chagataids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chagatai Khanate was the Mongol successor state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> ruled by the descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khan">Chagatai Khan</a> from the thirteenth century. It covered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and the lands east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> and gradually adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Its western part later fell under the control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>, while Chagatai khans continued to rule in the east.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate.jpg" alt="Chagatai Khanate" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chagatai_Khanate_map_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> died in 1227, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khan">Chagatai Khan</a> inherited lands that roughly matched the defunct Qara Khitai empire: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/issyk-kul">Issyk-Kul</a>, Ili, Chu and Talas regions, Transoxania and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>. Chagatai was not fully independent and still received orders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>. After his death in 1242 the throne passed between young khans, regents and appointees of rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khans</a>. With the division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> after 1259 the khanate became functionally separate, and at its height in the late 13th century it stretched from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai Mountains</a>. It was also called the Dumdadu Mongγol Ulus, the Middle Mongolian Empire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaidu">Kaidu</a>, who dominated the khanate from 1266 to 1301, it fought both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>. After Kaidu's death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duwa">Duwa</a> recognised Yuan authority in 1303. Chagatai armies raided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> several times, with little lasting result. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarmashirin">Tarmashirin</a>, khan from 1326 to 1334, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, but an anti-Muslim rising of the eastern tribes brought him down.</p><p>In the 1340s the khanate split in two. In Transoxania the khans became puppets of local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> and later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, who ruled from 1369. In the east, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dughlat">Dughlats</a> enthroned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlugh-timur">Tughlugh Timur</a> as khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a> in 1347, and he converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in 1350. Moghulistan lasted until the late 15th century, when it divided into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turpan-khanate">Turpan Khanates</a>. The last Chagatai domains lost their independence to the Dzungar Khanate in 1680.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Chagatai Mongols remained mostly nomadic in their mode of government and did not settle in towns until the late 15th century. They treated the townspeople of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> as dependants. The Mongol ruling groups underwent a gradual and uneven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkification">Turkification</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamization">Islamization</a>, though Mongolian political and administrative traditions stayed important, and Mongolian was still used for official documents into the 14th century. The Kumul Khanate, governed by descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khan">Chagatai Khan</a>, was abolished in 1930, which ended the dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chagatai Khanate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the succession war between Kublai and Ariq Böke (1260–1264), the Golden Horde, Ilkhanate, <b>Chagatai Khanate</b> and Yuan dynasty operated as largely independent states, though they continued to acknowledge a shared Chinggisid heritage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>He rose from the minor nobility of the Barlas tribe in Transoxiana (modern Uzbekistan), within the <b>Chagatai Khanate</b>, through military talent, political ruthlessness and skilful use of the Mongol framework of legitimacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire broke into four realms: the Yuan dynasty in China, which fell in 1368, the Ilkhanate in Persia, the <b>Chagatai Khanate</b> in Central Asia and the Golden Horde, whose successors, among them the Crimean and Kazakh khanates, lasted into the 18th century.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>It fought off the Mongol invasions from the <b>Chagatai Khanate</b> and carried the sultanate’s conquests deep into southern India.</blockquote><a href="/societies/delhi-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Delhi Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Mongols it remained a privileged vassal until rule passed to the <b>Chagatai Khanate</b> in the 14th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-qocho">Kingdom of Qocho</a></li><li><blockquote>When the <b>Chagatai Khanate</b> split in the 1340s, the Dughlat amir Puladchi enthroned Tughlugh Timur, a claimed descendant of Chagatai, as khan of the eastern part in 1347.</blockquote><a href="/state/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a></li></ul></section>
